# The file tree is a window: once `Ctrl-W h` has put focus in it, `Ctrl-W
# w` cycles on to the next window and `Ctrl-W l` moves right into the
# editor — the same way `Ctrl-L` does, for hands that only know `Ctrl-W`.
# The tree's cursor sits on a folder row, so `l` on its own would expand
# it, not leave the tree; `x` shows where the focus is (the tree ignores
# it, the editor deletes a char).
write sub/b.txt "x\n"
write a.txt "alpha\n"
open a.txt
command editor.use_vim

key ctrl+w
key h
key up
type x
expect screen contains " 1 alpha"
key ctrl+w
key w
type x
expect screen contains " 1 lpha"
key ctrl+w
key h
key up
type x
expect screen contains " 1 lpha"
key ctrl+w
key l
type x
expect screen contains " 1 pha"
